db/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125528 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125528 #edb/ref 1061 30935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125528 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "87b55278-4bb6-5686-b4ef-178d5036b236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125528 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125529 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125529 #edb/ref 1061 30936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125529 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4850b0cc-54ab-5a26-9440-b0d1cf757680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125529 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125530 #edb/ref 1061 30937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125530 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd19c878-fabf-5b99-9507-9a32fb862324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125530 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125749 #edb/ref 1064 "We can consider $f(\\mathbf{r}(t))$ as the values taken by the function $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$ So, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))]\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the **rate of change of $f$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$**\n\nFurthermore, if $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is a level curve $f(x,y)=c,$ then by the chain rule we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =c \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(f(r(t))) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(c) \\\\ ∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can conclude that the gradient vector $\\nabla f$ is orthogonal to every level curve of $f.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125749 #edb/ref 1065 2652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125749 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "06350cc4-9c76-53db-a464-43e93f2fcc2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125749 #edb/ref 1067 "The Derivative as a Rate of Change" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125835 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have the function \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{r}) = f(x,y) = xy^2,\n$$\n\nwhich takes a vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x(t), y(t) \\right\\rangle = \\left\\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nas input. How do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right]?$\n\nTo do this, we use the **multivariable chain rule in vector form**, which states\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now apply this to our functions. First, we compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(f(x,y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(f(x,y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{y}^{2},2xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2},2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =-{sin}^{3}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125835 #edb/ref 1065 4967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125835 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "673ff670-99f1-5416-aa83-5c4c8bf0e4f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125835 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125836 #edb/ref 1064 "The chain rule for a function $f(\\mathbf{r}),$ where $\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x_1(t), x_2(t), \\ldots, x_m(t) \\right\\rangle$ is a vector-valued function of $m$ variables, is the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)\n$$\n\nHow does this chain rule in vector form relate to the chain rule we have seen before, which states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d f} {\\textrm d t} \t= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_1}{\\textrm d t}  \n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_2}{\\textrm d t} + \\dots + \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_m} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_m}{\\textrm d t}?\n$$\n\nLet's expand $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)$ to see. Recall that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))=⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{\\text{d}t}+⋯+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey are exactly the same, just different notations!\n\n**Note**: If we replace $\\mathbf{r}$ with a single-valued function $g(t),$ we get back the chain rule from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(g(t))] & =∇f(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125836 #edb/ref 1065 4968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125